Title: "Complex Integration and Cauchy's Theorem"
Id: "260b80fb-86c6-40ac-a46a-486164aea02b"


Text: "Integration in the complex plane takes place along directed curves
       (contours). Cauchy's integral theorem and formula demonstrate that values
       and derivatives of holomorphic functions inside a region are entirely
       determined by their boundary values."
Id: "a53b5226-2345-4a75-b62f-fb6784fa06fc"


SectionTitle: "Paths and Contour Integrals"
Id: "e1f3d3b0-7dd5-4d9d-bc94-62aa17e94846"


TextDefinition: "A path in an open set $U\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ is a continuous
                 map $\gamma:[a,b]\to U$. It is piecewise continuously
                 differentiable if $[a,b]$ can be divided into finitely many
                 subintervals on which $\gamma$ has a continuous derivative. The
                 path is closed when $\gamma(a)=\gamma(b)$. Its parameterization
                 includes orientation and multiplicity."
Id: "6298206c-1aa2-49a7-b2c9-6f92d0a831f8"


[\complex.path:on{a, b}]
Declares: gamma(t_) is \function:on{\reals}:to{\complex.numbers}
when: a, b is \real
Documented:
. called: "complex path on $[a?, b?]$"
. description: "A continuous parameterized curve
                $\gamma : [a, b] \to \mathbb{C}$."
Id: "28c63b3c-de18-42f8-b10b-04695ee6410a"


[\(closed)::complex.path:on{a, b}]
Refines: gamma
when: a, b is \real
specifies: gamma is \complex.path:on{a, b}
satisfies: gamma(a) = gamma(b)
Documented:
. adjective: "closed"
. description: "A complex path is closed if its endpoints coincide:
                $\gamma(a) = \gamma(b)$."
Id: "f3a6c8ea-28b0-46a1-ac84-0499c7b6a970"


[\contour.integral:of{f}:along{gamma}]
Defines: I is \complex
using: a, b is \real
when:
. gamma is \complex.path:on{a, b}
. f is \function:on{\complex.numbers}:to{\complex.numbers}
Documented:
. called: "contour integral of $f?$ along $\gamma?$"
. written: "\oint_{\gamma?} f?(z) \, dz"
. description: "The contour integral $\oint_\gamma f(z) dz$ along curve
                $\gamma$."
Id: "e6283364-d507-4dfb-bbeb-a2e09a538cef"


TextDefinition: "If $\gamma:[a,b]\to U$ is piecewise continuously differentiable
                 and $f:U\to\mathbb{C}$ is continuous, the contour integral is
                 $\int_\gamma f(z)\,dz=\int_a^b f(\gamma(t))\gamma'(t)\,dt$,
                 with the ordinary real integral applied to real and imaginary
                 parts. Orientation reversal changes the sign; an
                 orientation-preserving reparameterization does not change the
                 value."
Id: "ed8000df-3011-44fe-912c-fc7e8c6ad086"


SectionTitle: "Cauchy's Theorem and Residues"
Id: "03283fcd-c542-49a4-92a2-19ded30ed498"


TextTheorem: "Cauchy's integral theorem. Let $U\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ be open and
              simply connected, let $f:U\to\mathbb{C}$ be holomorphic, and let
              $\gamma$ be a closed piecewise continuously differentiable path in
              $U$. Then $\int_\gamma f(z)\,dz=0$. More generally, simple
              connectedness may be replaced by the requirement that $\gamma$ be
              null-homologous in $U$."
Id: "ba6ab675-4730-4cee-905c-ec081e05d378"


TextTheorem: "Cauchy's integral formula. Under the corresponding disk or
              winding-number hypotheses, if $z_0$ lies inside a positively
              oriented simple closed contour $\gamma$, then
              $f(z_0)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_\gamma
              \frac{f(z)}{z-z_0}\,dz$. Differentiating the formula yields
              $f^{(n)}(z_0)=\frac{n!}{2\pi i}\int_\gamma
              \frac{f(z)}{(z-z_0)^{n+1}}\,dz$."
Id: "6b44eb1b-43c8-497e-a408-348353760216"


[\residue:of{f}:at{z0}]
Defines: Res is \complex
when:
. z0 is \complex
. f is \function:on{\complex.numbers}:to{\complex.numbers}
Documented:
. called: "residue of $f?$ at $z0?$"
. written: "\operatorname{Res}(f?, z0?)"
. description: "The coefficient $a_{-1}$ in the Laurent expansion of $f$ around
                isolated singularity $z_0$."
Id: "19aa67b3-3856-40b0-8d20-8fae7dd0ec19"


TextTheorem: "Residue theorem. Let $U\subseteq\mathbb{C}$ be open, let
              $f$ be meromorphic on $U$, and let $\gamma$ be a closed
              piecewise smooth cycle in $U$ that passes through no pole and is
              null-homologous in $U$. If $a_1,\ldots,a_m$ are the poles around
              which $\gamma$ has nonzero winding number, then
              $\int_\gamma f(z)\,dz=2\pi i\sum_{k=1}^m
              \operatorname{Ind}(\gamma,a_k)\operatorname{Res}(f,a_k)$."
Id: "3069a9a0-50d1-4424-b9e6-783a50b65cb5"


Text: "The hypotheses prevent two distinct failures. Holomorphy away from
       isolated poles makes residues local and finite in number inside the
       relevant region. The homological condition prevents the contour from
       enclosing holes of the domain on which no primitive need exist. Omitting
       either condition changes the theorem, not merely its proof."
Id: "a97c24e5-9129-4b48-9d73-4caf54ce5cd1"
